I’ll first inspect the specimen metadata, diffraction grid, frozen reference index, and the local GSAS-II workflow guidance. Then I’ll build the simplest chemically supported multiphase model, verify that all coherent peak families are accounted for, and refine only stable physical parameters before exporting the replayable project and requested files.

The specimen is a Cu Kα reflection scan from 9.9–100.1° 2θ after heating WO₃ with Mn₃O₄ at 800 °C. The chemically ordinary first hypothesis is therefore manganese tungstate plus possible residual manganese oxide or tungsten oxide; I’m now extracting candidate records from the frozen index and checking the exact project/export workflow.

Peak positions and relative intensities match the P2/c wolframite MnWO₄ reference across the full range, including the characteristic paired maxima near 30.36/30.81° and families at 18.86, 24.05/24.61, 36.58, 51.98, 53.84, and 61–69°. I see no separate precursor family at this stage. I’m moving to a full-grid GSAS-II refinement and will inspect the post-fit residual before deciding whether any second phase is warranted.

The one-phase residual is not random: it contains a second broad reflection family at 20.32/20.88, 23.50, 24.74, 29.14, 33.66–35.62, 47.02, 54.89, and 55.85°. Screening the frozen same-chemistry references strongly favors residual WO₃, especially the orthorhombic Pnma record; manganese oxides score much worse and do not cover that family. I’m adding WO₃ as the unreacted precursor and comparing its exact-composition polymorphs from the same one-phase checkpoint.

The polymorph comparison is running from the same saved MnWO₄ checkpoint, with identical background, calibration, and profile assumptions. This isolates structure choice from refinement-path effects; I’m retaining each branch project so a numerically unstable candidate is treated as unknown rather than silently rejected.

The comparison is computation-heavy because each WO₃ polymorph is receiving four full-grid refinements. It is still progressing without an error; once the branches complete I’ll rank them by stable positive scale, family coverage, geometry, and Rwp—not by Rwp alone.

Adding monoclinic room-temperature WO₃ improves the fit and gives a stable positive fraction, but it exposes another strong, internally coherent family (notably 20.32/20.88, 29.15, 35.64, 47.02, and 54.94°). None of the available MnO/Mn₃O₄/Mn₂O₃ references covers that family broadly, so the inventory is not yet complete. I’m indexing this remaining family before considering any analogue transfer.

The remaining family indexes cleanly as a rhombohedral lattice (hexagonal setting a ≈ 8.903 Å, c ≈ 10.476 Å; M20 ≈ 201 with all selected lines indexed). The observed 003 reflection excludes R‑3c. The frozen index contains an isovalent, same-Mn R‑3 analogue, Mn₃TeO₆, whose known cell and stoichiometry point directly to R‑3 Mn₃WO₆ by Te⁶⁺→W⁶⁺ substitution. This is now a chemically supported cross-chemistry transfer, so I’m testing that explicit model against the full residual.

The R‑3 Mn₃WO₆ analogue is decisively supported: adding it drops full-grid Rwp from 18.0% to 5.26% immediately, and to 3.51% after stable cell/profile refinement. Current crystalline fractions are approximately 69.5% MnWO₄, 15.3% WO₃, and 15.1% Mn₃WO₆. I’m now checking that no coherent fourth family remains, then refining coordinates and specimen terms from this complete checkpoint with physical rollback checks.

The phase inventory is now coherent: MnWO₄, monoclinic WO₃, and rhombohedral Mn₃WO₆ account for every reflection family. The three-phase fit is already at Rwp ≈ 3.49%; I’m now refining coordinates and physically supported profile terms from that checkpoint, with geometry checks and rollback at each stage before producing the final replayable project.

The coordinate stages converged cleanly and reduced Rwp from 3.49% to 2.47%. Heavy-atom shifts are small and well determined; the WO₃ oxygen block moved more substantially, so I’m screening bond distances and polyhedra now before accepting that numerical improvement.

The unconstrained WO₃ oxygen shifts were indeed too large (≈0.27 Å RMS versus ≤0.05 Å expected reference mismatch), despite acceptable bond contacts. I have rolled that block back and am comparing two bounded coordinate windows; the accepted model will retain only the improvement supported without large coordinate drift.

The bounded tests confirm the light-atom problem: tighter and looser windows both drive many WO₃/Mn₃WO₆ oxygen coordinates to their bounds, while improving Rwp by only 0.01–0.05 percentage point after recomputation. I’m therefore retaining the published/analogue oxygen sublattices and the stable heavy-atom/MnWO₄ refinements, then moving to instrument and specimen terms that the full profile can actually determine.

The profile terms and one tied Uiso per phase are stable and physical; Rwp is now 2.10% (GOF 1.19). Texture screening shows no MnWO₄ effect and only a very weak WO₃ [010] signal; I’m checking its statistical stability before deciding whether that one extra parameter belongs in the final model.

The supported final complexity is now fixed: weak WO₃ [010] preferred orientation and a stable Mn₃WO₆ [010] uniaxial microstrain term; surface roughness was rejected because it worsened the fit and hit a bound. I’m generating the final CIFs/profile/project now, then I’ll replay the saved project independently and validate every output.

Replay validation succeeded with no physical-profile flags: the saved GPX regenerates the full 8,301-point pattern, preserves all three phase fractions exactly, and gives fresh-replay Rwp 2.07414%. The CIFs also pass symmetry and contact checks; I’m documenting the evidence and the analogue-derived Mn₃WO₆ uncertainty now.

